 after completing <1>physical therapy</1>.     4. Left buttock and anterior thigh pain, stable and likely due to <2>OA</2>. Recc heat, massage, and <3>ibuprofen</3>. This improved after completing <4>physical therapy</4>.     5. <5>Osteoporosis</5>, currently on *****. The patient's baseline <6>bone density scan</6> showed <7>osteoporosis</7> in the low back. She had a repeat <8>bone density scan</8> last July 2018 which showed slight improvement of bone density at the femur neck with <9>T-score</9> (-2.4) now in the <10>osteopenia</10> range. The <11>T-scores</11> of her lumbar spine and femur are stable from last year which is in the <12>osteopenia</12> range. Advised patient to continue <13>calcium</13> and <14>Vitamin D</14> supplements and to continue weight bearing exercises. ***** continue monitor <15>DEXA scan</15>. If the patient's <16>BMD</16> falls below -2.5, she will need to see a dentist, then we could start the her on <17>Prolia 60 mg SQ</17> every 6 months. We can check again her <18>DEXA</18> in 1 year to assess change, due July 2019.     6. <19>Headaches</19>, intermittent, chronic: Seek care if worsening. Advised to follow-up with <20>PCP</20>. If worsening, consider <21>brain MRI</21>.     7. <22>Loose stools</22>, grade 1, mostly after meals. Discussed trying daily <23>probiotics</23>.        Follow up in 6 months or sooner if she has new or worsening symptoms.     I spent a total of 40 minutes face-to-face with the patient and 30 minutes of that time was spent counseling regarding the treatment plan, symptoms and therapeutic options.         
