ner_results: ```plaintext
enhancement along the <1>sacral foramina</1> and <2>sacral canal</2>. <3>T12-L1</3>: Areas mild epidural <4>tumor</4> at <5>T12</5> compressing the <6>thecal sac</6> ventrally. <7>L1-L2</7>: Mild <8>facet hypertrophy</8> and mild <9>central stenosis</9>. <10>L2-L3</10>: Mild <11>dural thickening</11> and <12>facet hypertrophy</12>. <13>L3-L4</13>: Is asymmetrical <14>epidural fat</14> compressing the <15>thecal sac</15> with moderate <16>stenosis</16>. It is mild <17>dural enhancement</17>. There is mild <18>tumor enhancement</18> posterior to the <19>psoas muscle</19> on the right side at <20>L4</20>. Measures approximately <21>12 mm</21>. <22>L4-L5</22>: <23>Disc bulge</23> and right sided small <24>disc herniation</24> resulting in right lateral recess and <25>neural foraminal stenosis</25>. Mild <26>dural contrast enhancement</26>. <27>L5-S1</27>: There is no central <28>skull tumor</28>. There is <29>epidural thickening</29>. This is extending from <30>S1 segment</30>. <31>02/10/17 MRI Thoracic spine w/ and w/o contrast</31>: <32>Thoracic metastases</32> throughout the <33>spine</33>. Minimal compression of <34>T6</34>. There is <35>spinal CORD compression</35> due to <36>epidural tumor</36> and thickening from approximately <37>T5-T9</37>. There is separate mild <38>epidural tumor</38> and <39>thecal sac</39> and <40>cord deformity</40> at <41>T12</41>. The <42>epidural enhancement</42> is contiguous throughout the <43>thoracic spine</43>. <44>02/10/17 MRI Cervical spine w/ and w/o contrast</44>: <45>Heterogeneous marrow signal</45> throughout the <46>cervical spine</46> consistent with <47>metastatic replacement</47>. No <48>epidural tumor</48>. No <49>intraspinal meningeal contrast enhancement</49>. <50>C5-C6</50>: Mild <51>bulge</51> with mild <52>central stenosis</52>. <53>02/10/17 Bone scan</53>: Multiple focal areas of increased activity are visualized in the <54>skull</54>, <55>cervical</55>, <56>thoracic</56> and <57>lumbar spine</57>, multiple <58>ribs</58> bilaterally <59>sternum</59>, <60>pelvis</60>, proximal <61>humeri</61>, <62>hips</62> and <63>femurs</63>. Findings correlates with <64>lesions</64> seen on <65>MRI</65> and are consistent with widespread <66>metastatic involvement</66>. Of incidental note, there is no evidence of <67>mass abnormality</67> of the <68>kidneys</68> or <69>obstructive uropathy</69>. <70>02/10/17 MRI Brain</70>: Small <71>dural based metastases</71> are present along the <72>posterior interhemispheric falx</72> and <73>right parietal convexity</73>. These measure approximately <74>1 cm</74> or less.
