her future risk of recurrent <1>DCIS</1> or progression to <2>invasive ductal cancer</2> over next decade, given her overall excellent health and likelihood of living another 20 years. I informed her that, overall, patients with <3>DCIS</3> may have up to 25-30% recurrence risk which could be reduced to 15% or less if given breast <4>XRT</4>; and that adjuvant <5>endocrine therapy</5> (e.g. aromatase inhibitor like <6>Arimidex</6>) for 5 years after <7>XRT</7> would further reduce her risk to 5-8% and provide lifelong chemoprotectant benefit for her uninvolved (left) breast. I emphasized that these options would not likely enhance her longevity. I also pointed out that by the <8>MSKCC nomogram</8> for <9>DCIS recurrence risk</9>, inputting her age and all other features, her 10 year risk might be closer to 15%, but that <10>XRT</10> +/- <11>endocrine therapy</11> would provide significant risk reduction that she should consider further after her pending <12>UCSF Rad Onc consultation</12>. We also discussed the side effects and <13>MSS</13> and <14>bone density complications</14> of <15>aromatase inhibitor therapy</15> given her present diagnosis of <16>osteopenia</16>, and how this latter risk could be mitigated with <17>bisphosphonate therapy</17>. Plan: She is inclined toward undertaking additional adjuvant treatment(s) to minimize her risks over the next decade of recurrent <18>DCIS</18> or progression to <19>invasive breast cancer</19>. She understands and looks forward to her ***** evaluation in two weeks which requires her first decision. She will then plan to return for ***** follow-up visit in 3-4 months allowing time to complete right breast <20>XRT</20> if that option is recommended and accepted.
