explained to her that she now had clear evidence of invasive <1>breast cancer</1>, but noted that her <2>PET/CT</2> demonstrated no evidence of <3>metastases</3> and therefore she can now be treated with curative intent. I discussed with ***** and her husband in great detail the natural history and treatment of early stage *****-/*****+ <4>IDC</4>. I explained that were she to forego the recommended therapy, I am very concerned that she would develop <5>metastatic disease</5> in the short term, even in the context of her 10 year history of <6>DCIS</6>. We discussed the multimodality approach to <7>breast cancer treatment</7>. The patient understands that her treatment recommendations will include <8>chemotherapy</8>, <9>biological therapies</9> (<10>trastuzumab</10> and <11>pertuzumab</11>), <12>surgery</12>, and <13>radiation</13>. We discussed the advantages of offering <14>chemotherapy</14> in the <15>neoadjuvant setting</15> in order to increase the chances of <16>breast conserving surgery</16>, while concurrently allowing us to assess the response to treatment. I explained that in the context of her high-risk node-negative, ***** positive disease we would typically recommend that she receive either 12 cycles of weekly <17>paclitaxel</17> plus <18>trastuzumab</18>/<19>pertuzumab</19> (<20>THP</20>) followed by 4 cycles of <21>doxorubicin</21> plus <22>cyclophosphamide</22> (<23>AC</23>) every 2 weeks or 6 cycles of every 3 week <24>docetaxel</24>, <25>carboplatin</25>, <26>trastuzumab</26>, and <27>pertuzumab</27> (<28>TCHP</28>). I also explained that either regimen would be followed by every 3 week <29>trastuzumab</29> for a total of one year of <30>biological therapy</30>. We reviewed the potential side effects of both regimens. Given her extensive disease even in the absence of nodal involvement, I recommend that she proceed with an <31>anthracycline-containing regimen</31>, unless she is found to have substantial <32>cardiac dysfunction</32> on <33>TTE</33>. I specifically discussed with ***** the typical and less common side-effects associated with <34>THP/AC</34>, such as: <35>fatigue</35>, <36>nausea</36>, <37>myelosuppression</37>, <38>nail changes</38>, <39>alopecia</39>, <40>diarrhea</40>, and <41>constipation</41>. I stated that
