 serious, but uncommon side effect of ***** may be an immune-mediated reaction. When this side effect occurs, it affects primarily the <1>bowels</1>, <2>liver</2>, <3>skin</3>, <4>nerves</4> and the <5>endocrine system</5>. This immune reaction can occur during treatment but can also be seen weeks or months after discontinuation of treatment. Symptoms of this reaction will be monitored throughout treatment (<6>diarrhea</6>, <7>rash</7>, and <8>neuropathy</8>). Lab work will check for elevated <9>liver enzymes</9> and <10>thyroid function</10>.    Contact your health care provider right away if you have any new or worsening symptoms.    Not all side effects are listed above. Some that are rare (occurring in less than 10% of patients) are not listed here. However, you should always inform your health care provider if you experience any unusual symptoms.    <11>Gemzar</11> (<12>Gemcitabine</12>)    Side Effects:  Important things to remember about <13>Gemzar</13> side effects:    Most people do not experience all of the <14>Gemzar</14> side effects listed.  <15>Gemzar</15> side effects are often predictable in terms of their onset, duration and severity.  <16>Gemzar</16> side effects are almost always reversible and will go away after treatment is complete.  There are many options to help minimize or prevent <17>Gemzar</17> side effects.  There is no relationship between the presence or severity of <18>Gemzar</18> side effects and the effectiveness of <19>Gemzar</19>.    The following <20>Gemzar</20> side effects are common (occurring in more than 30%) for patients taking <21>Gemzar</21>:    <22>Flu-like symptoms</22> (<23>muscle pain</23>, <24>fever</24>, <25>headache</25>, <26>chills</26>, <27>fatigue</27>)  <28>Fever</28>  (within 6-12 hours of first dose)  <29>Fatigue</29>  <30>Nausea</30> (mild)  <31>Vomiting</31>  <32>Poor appetite</32>  <33>Skin rash</33>  <34>Low blood counts</34>.  Your <35>white and red blood cells</35> and <36>platelets</36> may temporarily 