ner_results: ```plaintext
WDWN, NAD  Eyes - sclera anicteric, PERRL, EOMI  HENT - sinuses nontender, nasal mucosa intact, pharynx without erythema, stomatitis, or thrush   Lymph Nodes - no cervical, supraclavicular, or axillary lymphadenopathy  Respiratory - resonant to percussion throughout, CTA bilaterally; no wheezes, rhonchi, or crackles  Cardiovascular - Normal heart rate, normal rhythm, no murmurs, no edema  Breast - no masses bilaterally  GI - Bowel sounds normal, soft; no tenderness, no distention, no HSM  Musculoskeletal - No tenderness over bones or joints.   Neurologic - Alert & oriented x 3, ambulates w/o difficulty, good strength throughout  Psychiatric - Mood stable; no HI, SI, hallucinations, paranoia, or delusions  Skin - warm without rashes or lesions       Studies:  No new studies at this time.    Assessment and Recommendations:  39 y.o. premenopausal patient with a recent diagnosis of a deleterious <1>BRCA2 mutation</1> and a <2>oligometastatic</2> <3>IDC</3> of the <4>left breast</4> involving <5>T6</5>, treated with <6>neoadjuvant AC/T</6> followed by <7>radiation</7> to <8>T6</8>, and most recently <9>bilateral mastectomies</9> with <10>left reconstruction</10> who should proceed with <11>post-mastectomy radiation</11> at this time.    I again discussed with ***** the natural history and treatment of <12>oligometastatic breast cancer</12> in a premenopausal patient.  We discussed again the results from her surgery.  She is aware that in general <13>metastatic breast cancer</13> is not curable, but treatable, with primary goals of prolongation of life, while maintaining a good quality of life without excessive toxicities.  I noted that <14>systemic medications</14> are the mainstay of treatment for <15>MBC</15>, but that in select cases of <16>oligometastatic disease</16> we consider using local therapies such as <17>surgery</17> and <18>radiation</18> as has been done in her case.    She will continue with <19>ovarian suppression</19> as well as an <20>aromatase inhibitor</20> (<21>letrozole</21>).  We will start <22>palbociclib</22> when she completes <23>radiation</23> and has recovered from the <24>SE</24>.
``` 
