ner_results: ```plaintext
administered in the adjuvant or <1>neoadjuvant settings</1> with equivalent benefits on long term outcomes outcomes (***** ***** *****; ***** ***** *****; ***** ***** *****). However there are certain advantages to the <2>neoadjuvant timing</2>. These include the opportunity to confirm the in ***** response, the powerful prognostic information provided by <3>pathologic response status</3> (***** ***** *****; ***** ***** *****; ***** ***** *****; ***** ***** *****; ***** ***** *****), and the opportunity for salvage therapies or clinical trials should there be extensive residual disease after <4>neoadjuvant chemotherapy</4> ***** a high risk of relapse. She has already started on <5>neo-adjuvant AC/taxol chemotherapy</5> and I concurred with this (***** ***** *****; ***** ***** *****). This consists of <6>4 cycles of AC</6> at <7>60/600 mg/m2</7> given every <8>2 weeks</8> with <9>growth factor support</9> followed by <10>taxol</10> given either in <11>4 doses of 175mg/m2 q2weeks</11> or in <12>12 weekly doses of 80mg/m2</12>. Side effects were discussed including a potential for <13>nausea</13>, <14>vomiting</14>, <15>alopecia</15>, <16>immune suppression</16> and <17>infections</17>, <18>fatigue</18>, <19>neuropathy</19>. Long term sequelae include <20>1-2% incidence of cardiomyopathy</20> (***** ***** *****; ***** ***** *****) and <21>0.5% incidence of secondary myeloid neoplasms</21> (***** ***** *****). ***** will be having <22>surgery</22> after the completion of <23>neo-adjuvant chemotherapy</23>. Considering her <24>locally advanced disease</24>, it may be wise to perform an <25>axillary dissection</25>, and if feasible, to identify and remove the <26>subpectoral LNs</26> as well. The feasibility of this is best determined by ***** and may require a <27>repeat scan post-chemo</27> to determine the extent of residual macroscopic disease. ***** refer to ***** at ***** should she wish to pursue <28>surgery</28> here. ***** will benefit from <29>adjuvant hormonal therapy</29> for a duration of up to <30>10 years</30>. Both <31>tamoxifen</31> and <32>AIs</32> are effective with <33>AIs</33> showing superior efficacy.
``` 
