ner_results: ```plaintext
is mild <1>atherosclerotic calcification</1> of the <2>carotid arteries</2>, left slightly greater than right. There is no discrete or intense <3>thyroid hypermetabolism</3>, nor intensely hypermetabolic <4>adenopathy</4> within the neck. Thorax: There is persistent subtle irregularity/nodularity of the <5>bilateral breast tissues</5>, similar to prior study. In particular, there is an approximately <6>1.4 cm faintly hypermetabolic density</6> within the <7>right lateral breast</7> on <8>CT images 119-*****</8> with <9>SUV 1.0</9> (previously similar in size with <10>SUV 0.8</10>). There are other very faintly hypermetabolic nodular densities within the <11>both breasts</11>, including an approximately <12>1 cm density</12> within the <13>right superolateral breast</13> (<14>CT image 104</14>, <15>SUV 1.2</15>) and an approximately <16>2 cm elongated density</16> within the <17>left lateral breast</17> (<18>CT images 124-*****</18>, <19>SUV 1.3</19>). These findings also appear relatively stable from prior study. There are no definitive new or intensely hypermetabolic breast lesions on either side. The numerous hypermetabolic <20>right axillary</20> and <21>right subpectoral lymph nodes</21> seen previously has significantly improved with interval therapy. For example, there is a residual <22>2.1 x 1.0 cm right axillary node</22> with an internal <23>biopsy clip/marker</23> on <24>CT image 90</24> with <25>SUV 1.9</25> (previously <26>2.3 x 1.5 cm</26> with <27>SUV 15.1</27>), as well as a residual <28>1.2 x 1.0 cm right lateral subpectoral node</28> on <29>CT images 83-84</29> with <30>SUV 1.8</30> (previously <31>1.8 x 1.6 cm</31> with <32>SUV 9.6</32>). There are other scattered residual <33>right axillary</33> and <34>right subpectoral nodes</34> that have similarly significantly declined in size from prior study, and now demonstrate little (if any) appreciable metabolic activity. These findings are consistent with extensive <35>locoregional nodal metastatic disease</35> that has substantially improved with interval therapy. Scattered small, faintly hypermetabolic <36>left axillary lymph nodes</36> are most likely benign/reactive.
