hypermetabolic internal mammary, hilar, or mediastinal <1>adenopathy</1>. There is a persistent <2>Port-A-Cath</2> within the left anterior upper thoracic wall (distal catheter tip near *****/right atrial junction). Within the lungs, there are scattered areas of presumed <3>atelectasis</3> or <4>inflammation</4>, particularly in the right thoracic paravertebral region (e.g. on <5>CT images 77-80</5> and <6>79-102</6>), as well as presumed atelectasis or mild inflammation within the <7>lingula</7>. Conversely, a prior hazy/ground-glass <8>opacity</8> within the posterior right lower lobe has diminished or resolved. There are probable tiny, partially-calcified <9>scars</9>/<10>granulomas</10> within the left apex laterally (<11>CT image 76</11>) and at the left posterior lung base (<12>CT image 119</12>), also seen previously. There is mild probable left apical <13>scarring</13> (<14>CT image 71</14>), as well as a suspected tiny (3-4 mm) <15>nodule</15> within the right medial lung apex (<16>CT image 74</16>). There is significant respiratory motion, limiting evaluation for other potential small/tiny lung nodules. However, there are no discretely or intensely hypermetabolic lung nodules or masses. There are no substantial <17>pleural</17> or <18>pericardial effusions</18>. There is suggestion of a small <19>hiatal hernia</19>. The heart appears borderline/mildly enlarged, with mild coronary arterial <20>atherosclerotic calcification</20>. Abdomen and Pelvis: There is physiologic hepatic and splenic metabolic activity, with mildly asymmetric elevation of the right <21>hemidiaphragm</21>. The <22>gallbladder</22> is not well evaluated on this study, with suspected <23>cholelithiasis</23> (better seen previously). There is no significant abnormal adrenal or pancreatic hypermetabolism. There is no evidence of an <24>abdominal aortic aneurysm</24>. There is physiologic GI activity, with relatively prominent, non-discrete proximal colonic activity most likely reflecting normal variation. There is extensive <25>stool</25> within the <26>rectum</26>. There is physiologic
