ner_results: ```plaintext
urinary radiotracer excretion, with moderate bilateral renal cortical <1>scarring</1>. There is no definitive abnormal uterine hypermetabolism. The lower pelvis is again suboptimally visualized on low-dose <2>CT</2> due to extensive streak artifacts from the patient's right hip <3>prosthesis</3>. Small bilateral inguinal lymph nodes are likely benign/reactive, with no intensely hypermetabolic subdiaphragmatic <4>adenopathy</4>. Musculoskeletal: There is relatively prominent muscle tension/strain in the left subscapular region and adjacent to both hips. There are other scattered areas of presumed subcutaneous inflammation or edema, particularly within the patient's pannus bilaterally, lower back, and right lateral upper thigh. There is also more prominent stranding/inflammation of the subcutaneous fat in the left lateral abdominal and flank regions (e.g. on <5>CT images 134-*****</5>). This is new from prior study, and may reflect interval trauma to this region. There are chronic degenerative changes of the skeleton, with stable mild spinal <6>scoliosis</6>. There is a persistent indwelling right hip <7>prosthesis</7>. There are persistent post-surgical changes within the lower lumbar spine and left humeral head, as well as probable post-surgical changes of the right posterior ilium (e.g. a bone graft donor site). There are probable old/healed rib injuries (e.g. within the right posterior ribcage), as well as probable new traumatic injury/fracture of the left 12th rib (<8>SUV 3.7</8>). Prior activity near the left posterior acetabulum/ischium has resolved. A prior discrete focus of hypermetabolism within the right inferior L5 vertebra (near the right lumbosacral junction) has also resolved, as has prior hypermetabolism within the left posterior third rib and right posterior ninth rib. These prior findings were equivocal, with limited, treated/resolved (by metabolism) skeletal metastatic disease not entirely excluded.
