had a pathologic diagnosis of <1>metastatic disease</1>, but was instead started on treatment in the form of <2>fulvestrant</2>. A followup <3>scan</3> in <4>August 2012</4> showed increase in size of the <5>lung lesion</5> but modest decrease in the size of the <6>mediastinal lymphadenopathy</6>. Based on the increasing <7>chest pain</7> being experienced by the patient, I am highly suspicious that the <8>lung lesion</8> is progressing further, and the <9>subpleural thickening</9> in the <10>anterior left lower lobe</10> likely represents progression of <11>disease</11> as well. At this point, in order to make the most appropriate treatment recommendations, I fully agree with the ***** medical oncology group that the patient needs to have a <12>biopsy</12> of either the <13>pulmonary nodule</13>, <14>mediastinal adenopathy</14>, or the area of <15>subpleural thickening</15> that might represent another site of <16>metastatic disease</16>. I explained to the patient that I do not know what is the ***** approach to <17>biopsy</17> one or more of these <18>lesions</18>, and that we will need a followup <19>CT scan</19> which has <20>contrast</20> and <21>thin slices</21> in order to better understand the extent of <22>disease</22>, since the <23>CT scan</23> from <24>08/18/2012</24> was <25>noncontrast</25> and used for <26>anatomic localization</26> only. In the meantime, I have submitted a referral to both <27>interventional radiology</27> as well as <28>thoracic surgery</28> to determine what is the best route for <29>biopsy</29> of her <30>disease</30>. The patient understands that I cannot make a comprehensive treatment recommendation until I know the biological characteristics of her <31>metastatic disease</31>. If her <32>cancer</32> remains <33>estrogen receptor positive</33>, it would be reasonable to consider ongoing <34>hormonal therapy</34>, but most likely combined with a <35>biological therapy</35> such as the combination of <36>Exemestane</36> and <37>Afinitor</37>. We would also entertain the possibility of a <38>chemotherapy</38> such as <39>Xeloda</39> which could have reasonable efficacy as well as allow the patient to maintain a decent quality of life. Alternatively, if the <40>metastatic disease</40> is <41>hormone receptor</41>
